===== docs/AI_SYSTEM.md =====
    1: # AI Development System
    2: 
    3: M3 Memory uses coordinated AI-assisted development across Claude Code and OpenAI Codex.
    4: 
    5: Primary invariant: whoever builds should not be the only reviewer.
    6: 
    7: If Codex builds, Claude reviews. If Claude builds, Codex reviews.
    8: 
    9: ## Standard Lifecycle
   10: 
   11: For substantial work:
   12: 
   13: 1. Request
   14: 2. Classify task
   15: 3. Ponytail / scope check
   16: 4. Graphify repository context
   17: 5. Primary model plan
   18: 6. Second model adversarial review
   19: 7. Plan revision
   20: 8. Build
   21: 9. Second model cross-inspection
   22: 10. Test
   23: 11. Fix if required
   24: 12. Final report
   25: 
   26: Not every tiny task needs the full expensive loop.
   27: 
   28: ## Cost-Aware Task Tiers
   29: 
   30: Start with the lowest-cost model reasonably capable of the task. Escalate when confidence is low, tests fail repeatedly, architecture is ambiguous, security or data integrity is involved, historical evidence could be altered, or the cheaper model cannot solve the problem after a reasonable attempt.
   31: 
   32: Do not burn premium reasoning on mechanical work. Do not keep retrying a cheap model indefinitely when it is clearly failing.
   33: 
   34: Before substantial work, declare:
   35: 
   36: - Task tier
   37: - Risk areas touched
   38: - Required reviewer
   39: - Graphify checked
   40: - Ponytail checked
   41: 
   42: ### Tier 0: Mechanical / Cheap
   43: 
   44: Use the cheapest competent model available. Do not invoke Claude + Codex cross-review.
   45: 
   46: Examples:
   47: 
   48: - formatting
   49: - renaming
   50: - simple documentation cleanup
   51: - boilerplate
   52: - repetitive edits
   53: - straightforward file creation
   54: - simple test-data generation
   55: - lint fixes where the fix is obvious
   56: 
   57: ### Tier 1: Normal
   58: 
   59: Use a cost-efficient coding model. Cross-model review is optional. Escalate only when uncertainty or risk appears.
   60: 
   61: Examples:
   62: 
   63: - ordinary components
   64: - simple UI
   65: - routine CRUD
   66: - straightforward tests
   67: - README changes
   68: - common refactors
   69: 
   70: ### Tier 2: Important
   71: 
   72: Use a stronger coding/reasoning model. Builder and reviewer should be different models when practical.
   73: 
   74: Examples:
   75: 
   76: - meaningful application features
   77: - database queries
   78: - submission workflow
   79: - admin workflow
   80: - significant refactors
   81: - media handling
   82: 
   83: ### Tier 3: Critical
   84: 
   85: Use the strongest appropriate models. Never route these tasks to a weak model solely to save usage.
   86: 
   87: Examples:
   88: 
   89: - authentication
   90: - authorization
   91: - Row Level Security
   92: - database schema/migrations affecting historical records
   93: - contributor privacy
   94: - historical-source mutation
   95: - deletion behavior
   96: - upload security
   97: - secrets
   98: - production deployment permissions
   99: - major architectural changes
  100: 
  101: Required lifecycle:
  102: 
  103: 1. Primary model
  104: 2. Second model adversarial review
  105: 3. Revision
  106: 4. Implementation
  107: 5. Other model code review
  108: 6. Test
  109: 
  110: ## Tool Roles
  111: 
  112: - Claudex Loop: bounded cross-provider plan review, build, inspection, and preserved logs.
  113: - Ponytail: anti-bloat and anti-overengineering scope gate.
  114: - Graphify: repository knowledge graph for efficient agent context.
  115: - OmniRoute: future model routing and fallback layer. Keep it separate from the direct Claude/Codex loop until routing, fallback, failure behavior, logs, and provider authentication are proven.
  116: 
  117: ## Logs
  118: 
  119: Preserve plans and reviews under `docs/ai-runs/`.
  120: 
  121: Use identifiable model roles:
  122: 
  123: - `claude-planner`
  124: - `claude-builder`
  125: - `claude-reviewer`
  126: - `codex-planner`
  127: - `codex-builder`
  128: - `codex-reviewer`
  129: 
  130: Do not mark a cross-model workflow successful unless both models actually ran and their outputs were captured.
  131: 
  132: ## OmniRoute Policy
  133: 
  134: Subscription-authenticated services and API-billed services are distinct unless official tool documentation proves otherwise.
  135: 
  136: Never automatically downgrade security-, data-integrity-, privacy-, or architecture-sensitive tasks to a weaker model simply because it is cheaper.
  137: 
  138: OmniRoute may be tested independently after direct Claude/Codex operation works. It must not be inserted into the main orchestration path until routing, fallback, failure behavior, logs, and provider authentication are verified.
  139: 
  140: OmniRoute negative findings are operational guardrails, not dead documentation. Keep a concise record of failed installs, unsafe defaults, and deferred routing rules so future sessions do not repeat them.
